00:08:19.571 --> 00:08:20.796
listen, you're (beep)?

00:08:20.796 --> 00:08:21.860
(laughter)

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:22.707
What have you done here?

00:08:22.707 --> 00:08:23.635
- I remember that point.

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:25.267
It was a great moment in my life.

00:08:25.267 --> 00:08:27.184
- Who comes to see you?

00:08:28.616 --> 00:08:29.490
- They summon you.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.199
I got flown out to L.A.
(laughter)

00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:32.032
- Nice.

00:08:32.032 --> 00:08:34.006
Are you thinking, wow,
I'm getting flown to L.A.

00:08:34.006 --> 00:08:35.156
- First class.
- They don't even tell you.

00:08:35.156 --> 00:08:36.872
- First class. I have no idea.

00:08:36.872 --> 00:08:38.486
Well I kind of smelled somethin,

00:08:38.486 --> 00:08:40.192
but they took me to Paramount.

00:08:40.192 --> 00:08:41.025
You know those gates.

00:08:41.025 --> 00:08:42.012
It's like you feel like that

00:08:42.012 --> 00:08:42.966
you're going to the White House.

00:08:42.966 --> 00:08:44.458
And I went to John Goldwyn's office.

00:08:44.458 --> 00:08:46.247
He was Samuel Goldwyn's grandson.

00:08:46.247 --> 00:08:47.310
- He's a big guy. I know that.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.143
- Yeah.

00:08:48.143 --> 00:08:49.196
And so I'm in his office.

00:08:49.196 --> 00:08:50.925
I'm in the most important
office at Paramount.

00:08:50.925 --> 00:08:52.262
He was running the studio at the time

00:08:52.262 --> 00:08:53.452
and he just ...

00:08:53.452 --> 00:08:56.249
I remember I walked in
and he's shaking a little

00:08:56.249 --> 00:08:57.428
because he's so mad.

00:08:57.428 --> 00:08:58.717
And he just almost,

00:08:58.717 --> 00:09:00.539
everybody was there that
was involved in the movie.

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:02.429
He just unloaded on me and said,

00:09:02.429 --> 00:09:05.102
"this movie is unreleasable! (laughter)

00:09:05.102 --> 00:09:07.180
You have wasted my money!

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.102
This is irresponsible.

00:09:09.102 --> 00:09:10.578
I have a problem with everyone here."

00:09:10.578 --> 00:09:12.855
And then this guy who was
the President of M.T.V Films

00:09:12.855 --> 00:09:14.016
who they said,

00:09:14.016 --> 00:09:15.873
"well John, I don't think
you really got the film."

00:09:15.873 --> 00:09:17.444
"How dare you say that to me!"

00:09:17.444 --> 00:09:19.635
- [Host] Ohh god!
And starts screaming at

00:09:19.635 --> 00:09:20.860
company presidents cause of my movie.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:21.693
- [Woman] Wow!

00:09:21.693 --> 00:09:22.880
- There was nothing
redeemable about the work.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.072
Now did
- Not to him but

00:09:24.072 --> 00:09:25.246
here's the thing is to me,
- [Host] How do you go on in

00:09:25.246 --> 00:09:26.504
show business after something like that?

00:09:26.504 --> 00:09:27.817
- Well that, I'll never
forget that moment.

00:09:27.817 --> 00:09:28.650
Cause I feel my whole ...

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.320
All I wanted to do is
make movies at the time

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.663
and it was being destroyed

00:09:31.663 --> 00:09:32.899
(laughter)

00:09:32.899 --> 00:09:34.297
by Samuel Goldwyn's grandson.

00:09:34.297 --> 00:09:35.425
- [Host] He knows something.

00:09:35.425 --> 00:09:36.491
- Yeah, a little bit.
- [Host] Right?

00:09:36.491 --> 00:09:37.561
- And I'm sitting there,

00:09:37.561 --> 00:09:38.931
but a part of me was outside of my body

00:09:38.931 --> 00:09:42.460
going, you are in the Paramount big office

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.528
being yelled at.

00:09:43.528 --> 00:09:45.134
But I said, how (beep) cool is this?

00:09:45.134 --> 00:09:46.030
(laughter)

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:47.365
- Really? You were able to
- Yes.

00:09:47.365 --> 00:09:48.554
I was able to enjoy it.

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:49.549
- [Host] You turned it into a positive.

00:09:49.549 --> 00:09:50.549
- [Woman] Wow.

00:09:50.549 --> 00:09:51.715
- Absolutely, because who gets to have

00:09:51.715 --> 00:09:54.765
that very rare, beautiful
experience to get to heaven?

00:09:54.765 --> 00:09:56.609
- You know, I read How to Make
Friends and Influence People.

00:09:56.609 --> 00:09:58.415
But for you to have
such a positive attitude

00:09:58.415 --> 00:10:00.177
as your career is ...
- [Woman] That's amazing.

00:10:00.177 --> 00:10:01.704
- Well to me, the key is like

00:10:01.704 --> 00:10:04.170
surviving failure is a great ...

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.596
It's a very great experience to have.

00:10:07.596 --> 00:10:08.587
- [Host] Tell me about

00:10:08.587 --> 00:10:09.661
(laughter)

00:10:09.661 --> 00:10:11.235
- Yeah look, I went
through all of that Howard,

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:12.635
and the press ...

00:10:12.635 --> 00:10:15.770
I grew up watching
Siskell and Ebert on PBS,

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:17.563
you know when it was that show.

00:10:17.563 --> 00:10:19.634
And so I always dreamed when I was kid,

00:10:19.634 --> 00:10:22.353
someday Roger Ebert and
that other dude is dead,

00:10:22.353 --> 00:10:23.959
they're going to look
at me through the camera

00:10:23.959 --> 00:10:25.470
and say something about my movie.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:27.432
And here was Roger Ebert saying,

00:10:27.432 --> 00:10:30.009
"Pootie Tang is so bad I don't
even understand what it is.

00:10:30.009 --> 00:10:31.373
This isn't even a movie.

00:10:31.373 --> 00:10:32.463
It's not even complete."

00:10:32.463 --> 00:10:33.423
(laughter)

00:10:33.423 --> 00:10:34.796
- [Howard] But like he's like,

00:10:34.796 --> 00:10:36.012
why don't they shoot the director?

00:10:36.012 --> 00:10:36.845
(laughter)

00:10:36.845 --> 00:10:37.680
- Yeah, he was like so hateful.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.634
And I'm having the experience of

00:10:39.634 --> 00:10:41.593
becoming famous for making a movie

00:10:41.593 --> 00:10:43.799
everyone unanimously thinks is awful.

00:10:43.799 --> 00:10:45.987
There are some people who like to just

00:10:45.987 --> 00:10:47.657
show up and do a show and get a check.

00:10:47.657 --> 00:10:48.626
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:10:48.626 --> 00:10:49.790
- And they don't think about,

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.181
like I'm interested in everything.

00:10:51.181 --> 00:10:53.449
Like when I tour and they tell me,

00:10:53.449 --> 00:10:56.271
hey you're doing the Chicago Theater

00:10:56.271 --> 00:10:57.861
and here's how much you're making,

00:10:57.861 --> 00:10:59.567
they tell me this big exciting number.

00:10:59.567 --> 00:11:01.263
Then I go, I ask them,

00:11:01.263 --> 00:11:02.424
well how much are the tickets?

00:11:02.424 --> 00:11:04.921
And then they go well,
in order to pay you that

00:11:04.921 --> 00:11:06.448
they're charging 50 bucks a ticket.

00:11:06.448 --> 00:11:09.041
I don't want people to pay
50 bucks a ticket to see me.

00:11:09.041 --> 00:11:10.809
How much do I get paid

00:11:10.809 --> 00:11:13.286
if they pay 30 bucks a ticket?

00:11:13.286 --> 00:11:15.627
Well geez, it's only this.

00:11:15.627 --> 00:11:17.117
And I'm like, well that's more money

00:11:17.117 --> 00:11:18.499
than my dad made in his life.

00:11:18.499 --> 00:11:19.599
(laughter)

00:11:19.599 --> 00:11:20.512
I mean, I'll take it.

00:11:20.512 --> 00:11:21.345
And also,

00:11:21.345 --> 00:11:23.021
once I started looking at it that way,

00:11:23.021 --> 00:11:24.227
I learned stuff.

00:11:24.227 --> 00:11:25.813
Like I learned that if you ask for a

00:11:25.813 --> 00:11:28.213
high guarantee for a live show,

00:11:28.213 --> 00:11:31.477
you drive up the ticket price
cause the risk is higher.

00:11:31.477 --> 00:11:33.411
But if you tell the theater, look,

00:11:33.411 --> 00:11:34.677
don't guarantee me anything.

00:11:34.677 --> 00:11:37.458
Only pay me if we make money.

00:11:37.458 --> 00:11:40.534
Then the price of the tickets come down

00:11:40.534 --> 00:11:42.628
and they don't have to advertise as much

00:11:42.628 --> 00:11:44.876
because they're not as afraid
that they're going to lose ...

00:11:44.876 --> 00:11:46.215
Do you understand what I'm saying?

00:11:46.215 --> 00:11:47.048
- Yeah, totally.

00:11:47.048 --> 00:11:50.757
- So and if you work in a
theater without a guarantee,

00:11:50.757 --> 00:11:52.254
you actually get paid more

00:11:52.254 --> 00:11:54.812
because the show costs less.

00:11:54.812 --> 00:11:59.383
It costs less for the
advertising, all that stuff.

00:11:59.383 --> 00:12:00.962
So if you sell it out,

00:12:00.962 --> 00:12:02.640
you will make more money.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.177
So I started learning about that stuff.

00:12:04.177 --> 00:12:05.609
Like, really interesting to me.

00:12:05.609 --> 00:12:07.922
Like the story I tell on Conan about

00:12:07.922 --> 00:12:10.114
the guy next to me on the airplane

00:12:10.114 --> 00:12:12.841
when the internet shuts down suddenly.

00:12:12.841 --> 00:12:14.176
There wasn't anybody
next to me on that plane.

00:12:14.176 --> 00:12:15.192
That was me.

00:12:15.192 --> 00:12:16.672
There was nobody next to me.

00:12:16.672 --> 00:12:18.606
People don't talk to me on airplanes.

00:12:18.606 --> 00:12:20.225
Anytime you see me doing a bit

00:12:20.225 --> 00:12:21.920
where some stranger said something to me,

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:23.272
it's me.

00:12:23.272 --> 00:12:25.577
The fact is, I was really upset

00:12:25.577 --> 00:12:27.087
that the internet shut down.

00:12:27.087 --> 00:12:28.746
I was livid.

00:12:28.746 --> 00:12:31.551
And then I caught myself and said,

00:12:31.551 --> 00:12:34.836
wait a minute, what are you upset about?

00:12:34.836 --> 00:12:37.965
This was incredible that
this was possible even.

00:12:37.965 --> 00:12:39.657
And I sort of started,

00:12:39.657 --> 00:12:41.177
it was kind of like an epiphany for me.

00:12:41.177 --> 00:12:42.466
I started seeing things differently,

00:12:42.466 --> 00:12:45.077
like realizing that when we get to a gate

00:12:45.077 --> 00:12:46.841
and they go we need ten minutes because

00:12:46.841 --> 00:12:47.842
the guy at the gate,

00:12:47.842 --> 00:12:50.685
the plane at our gate
hasn't backed out yet.

00:12:50.685 --> 00:12:53.180
And I go, okay, come on.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:54.266
Like that was me.

00:12:54.266 --> 00:12:55.266
Let's go!

00:12:56.221 --> 00:12:57.782
Why?

00:12:57.782 --> 00:12:59.726
It's actually not going
to get me there quicker.

00:12:59.726 --> 00:13:00.884
It's not going to get,
you know what I mean?

00:13:00.884 --> 00:13:02.631
Like I'm losing nothing.

00:13:02.631 --> 00:13:04.375
I just feel bad.

00:13:04.375 --> 00:13:06.807
But if you can just like
take a breath and calm down

00:13:06.807 --> 00:13:08.543
and go, you know this is as good

00:13:08.543 --> 00:13:09.837
as they make these right now.

00:13:09.837 --> 00:13:12.188
This is as good as it is.

00:13:12.188 --> 00:13:14.830
And it's pretty goddamned great.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.879
But somehow, we don't, ehh.

00:13:16.879 --> 00:13:18.752
Doesn't make us happy, and that's too bad.

00:13:18.752 --> 00:13:21.249
I always think that means
people are bad people.

00:13:21.249 --> 00:13:23.479
It means that they're unhappy.

00:13:23.479 --> 00:13:24.991
- They just want jetpacks.

00:13:24.991 --> 00:13:26.158
That they have all this great (beep),

00:13:26.158 --> 00:13:27.542
and if you had a jet pack, you'd be like,

00:13:27.542 --> 00:13:28.915
I have the (beep) jet pack.

00:13:28.915 --> 00:13:30.660
I can't believe I got this jet pack.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.157
Who's your service provider
on your jet pack, cause ...

00:13:34.157 --> 00:13:36.339
Do they make the new one?

00:13:36.339 --> 00:13:38.545
I hate this thing.

00:13:38.545 --> 00:13:39.848
It sucks.

00:13:39.848 --> 00:13:41.526
And then we'd all sit
there and talk about that.

00:13:41.526 --> 00:13:42.485
Part of me feels like

00:13:42.485 --> 00:13:44.354
that if people have to go
back to basics a little bit.

00:13:44.354 --> 00:13:46.008
They'll start to see that,

00:13:46.008 --> 00:13:47.443
cause there's no ...

00:13:47.443 --> 00:13:49.440
You have a job and then you don't even

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.645
make enough for the (beep) you even own.

00:13:51.645 --> 00:13:54.798
You have a house full
and a car and a house

00:13:54.798 --> 00:13:56.702
of all this (beep) you can't have.

00:13:56.702 --> 00:13:57.869
You can't afford it.

00:13:57.869 --> 00:13:59.557
And you don't even get
the pride of saying like,

00:13:59.557 --> 00:14:01.166
I have this job and because of it

00:14:01.166 --> 00:14:02.284
I have that car.

00:14:02.284 --> 00:14:03.550
I have this job and because of it

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:05.130
I have custody of this car

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:08.428
that the bank is paying somebody else for.

00:14:08.428 --> 00:14:10.111
And if they ever come callin,

00:14:10.111 --> 00:14:12.421
I'm going to go to jail.

00:14:12.421 --> 00:14:14.557
It's really a (beep) life I think

00:14:14.557 --> 00:14:16.119
that we're living right now.

00:14:16.119 --> 00:14:17.261
And I feel bad for people,

00:14:17.261 --> 00:14:19.020
cause I see it in their
faces all the time.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.888
So that's really more what it was about.

00:14:20.888 --> 00:14:23.804
I didn't mean to say
we're a (beep) generation.

00:14:23.804 --> 00:14:27.793
I meant to say that we're
a (beep) generation.

00:14:27.793 --> 00:14:29.558
- So you never got
worried about tapping out?

00:14:29.558 --> 00:14:30.852
- Yeah, of course.

00:14:30.852 --> 00:14:32.185
I'm terrified of tapping out.

00:14:32.185 --> 00:14:33.093
- Yeah.

00:14:33.093 --> 00:14:34.753
- But that's a motivating fear.

00:14:34.753 --> 00:14:35.958
That's pressure.

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:36.791
- Yeah.

00:14:36.791 --> 00:14:37.799
- I like pressure.

00:14:37.799 --> 00:14:40.179
I learned that from Billie Jean King.

00:14:40.179 --> 00:14:43.035
- Billie Jean King!
- One of my heroes.

00:14:43.035 --> 00:14:44.026
Yes. One of my heroes.
- Really?

00:14:44.026 --> 00:14:45.037
Yes.

00:14:45.037 --> 00:14:48.195
I remember as a kid
watching a press conference

00:14:48.195 --> 00:14:50.425
with Billie Jean King and Bobby Riggs

00:14:50.425 --> 00:14:51.599
- [Interviewer] Yeah.
- When they were doing

00:14:51.599 --> 00:14:52.615
Battle of the Sexes.

00:14:52.615 --> 00:14:54.223
I was so fascinated by that,

00:14:54.223 --> 00:14:55.646
cause I was raised by a single mom

00:14:55.646 --> 00:14:57.126
and I have three older sisters.

00:14:57.126 --> 00:14:59.068
So I've always identified with

00:14:59.068 --> 00:15:00.622
women taking care of (beep)

00:15:00.622 --> 00:15:02.230
and I never saw women as weak.

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:06.034
I only ever knew my strong
working single mother.

00:15:06.034 --> 00:15:08.362
So watching Billie Jean King

00:15:08.362 --> 00:15:10.381
field this horrible,

00:15:10.381 --> 00:15:12.798
just misogyny was fascinating

00:15:13.744 --> 00:15:15.367
cause of the way she carried it.

00:15:15.367 --> 00:15:16.661
That's what made it work.

00:15:16.661 --> 00:15:18.393
Cause he would sit there going, you know,

00:15:18.393 --> 00:15:20.863
why don't you all get back in the kitchen?

00:15:20.863 --> 00:15:23.142
- (laughing) That's right,
I forgot he did that.

00:15:23.142 --> 00:15:24.078
- It was amazing.

00:15:24.078 --> 00:15:24.911
- Yeah.

00:15:24.911 --> 00:15:27.021
- And he'd have two girls
in bikinis with big tits.

00:15:27.021 --> 00:15:31.106
I mean there's no bigger
insult to a working woman

00:15:31.106 --> 00:15:32.959
than chicks with tits.

00:15:32.959 --> 00:15:34.457
And he'd sit there and he'd go like,

00:15:34.457 --> 00:15:35.972
yeah, this is what a woman is.

00:15:35.972 --> 00:15:37.098
What's your problem or whatever.

00:15:37.098 --> 00:15:39.112
And the way she carried it was amazing.

00:15:39.112 --> 00:15:41.162
She didn't sit there and turn red

00:15:41.162 --> 00:15:43.071
and be like Wilma and get angry.

00:15:43.071 --> 00:15:45.353
She just sat there like this.

00:15:45.353 --> 00:15:46.967
And then they asked her,

00:15:46.967 --> 00:15:50.142
how do you feel when he says all this?

00:15:50.142 --> 00:15:51.632
And she smiled and she said,

00:15:51.632 --> 00:15:53.886
all it does is put a
lot of pressure on me.

00:15:53.886 --> 00:15:55.777
And I love pressure.

00:15:55.777 --> 00:15:57.593
And she had this big smile on her face.

00:15:57.593 --> 00:15:59.405
She said I love pressure.

00:15:59.405 --> 00:16:00.591
And I thought, oh my,

00:16:00.591 --> 00:16:01.424
I never forgot it.

00:16:01.424 --> 00:16:02.537
I thought, oh my god.

00:16:02.537 --> 00:16:03.480
How do you love,

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:04.466
what does that mean?

00:16:04.466 --> 00:16:06.063
Like I thought really
carefully about that.

00:16:06.063 --> 00:16:07.386
She really did.

00:16:07.386 --> 00:16:08.487
She was like come on,

00:16:08.487 --> 00:16:09.320
keep it comin.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:10.292
I love it. I love it.

00:16:10.292 --> 00:16:11.321
I want this pressure.

00:16:11.321 --> 00:16:15.972
Cause she knows how to turn
pressure into hard work.

00:16:15.972 --> 00:16:17.062
She knows how to,

00:16:17.062 --> 00:16:18.083
it motivates her.

00:16:18.083 --> 00:16:18.916
- Yeah.

00:16:18.916 --> 00:16:20.453
- She knows that the heat from pressure

00:16:20.453 --> 00:16:21.699
is going to make her work harder

00:16:21.699 --> 00:16:22.908
so she's like, go ahead.

00:16:22.908 --> 00:16:26.554
The more he said the more she had to win.

